lena: Heads-up — docs PR #2411 (rate limiting / retry examples) is blocked by an API semantics question. Need clarification from runtime before we can ship the migration guide.

oscar: what's the unknown? quick summary pls.

lena: the PR shows a JS SDK snippet that retries on 429 and inspects the `Retry-After` header. But our Dedicated endpoints sometimes return `retry_after_ms` in the JSON body and omit `Retry-After` header. Hosted returns standard `Retry-After` header plus X-RateLimit-* headers. The docs currently only mention `Retry-After` header which is ambiguous for Dedicated customers.

harper: ah — so docs should state precedence and exact fields for both Hosted and Dedicated. Do we have a canonical list?

docs-reviewer-rajesh: yes please. reviewers keep asking if SDK should look at response body, header, or both. If we don't specify, customers will implement inconsistent clients.

lena: exact questions to runtime: 1) canonical precedence (header vs body) 2) units for `retry_after_ms` vs `Retry-After` (seconds vs ms) 3) are X-RateLimit-Remaining and X-RateLimit-Limit guaranteed across Hosted/Dedicated?

runtime-bot: runtime note: current deployed behavior — Hosted: includes `Retry-After` header (seconds) and X-RateLimit-Limit / X-RateLimit-Remaining. Dedicated: returns 429 with JSON body {"error":"rate_limited","retry_after_ms":<ms>} and may omit `Retry-After` header in some older stacks. Not all Dedicated fleets include X-RateLimit headers.

oscar: ok, so docs should document both patterns and recommend client precedence. What do we recommend?

docs-reviewer-rajesh: recommended wording: "Clients SHOULD honor `Retry-After` header if present. If absent, clients MAY inspect `retry_after_ms` in the JSON body and convert to seconds for backoff. For best compatibility, clients SHOULD apply exponential backoff that is capped and still respects server-provided values as an upper bound."

harper: can we add a JS SDK snippet that shows the exact check order? reviewers want copy-paste code.

lena: adding now. draft snippet in PR:```javascript
// SDK retry helper (simplified)
async function handle429(response) {
  // prefer standard header
  const raHeader = response.headers.get('Retry-After')
  if (raHeader) {
    // header is in seconds (integer or HTTP-date but we expect seconds)
    return Number(raHeader)
  }
  // fallback to body field if present (ms)
  try {
    const body = await response.json()
    if (body && typeof body.retry_after_ms === 'number') {
      return Math.ceil(body.retry_after_ms / 1000)
    }
  } catch (e) {
    // ignore parse errors
  }
  // no server guidance, use exponential backoff heuristic
  return null
}
```

docs-reviewer-rajesh: looks good. Add a short curl example showing Dedicated response shape and a note that `retry_after_ms` is milliseconds. Also add a small callout: "SDKs will implement the header-first precedence and body-fallback behavior."

lena: adding curl example:```bash
curl -i -X POST https://dedicated.redwood.example/v1/generate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -d '{"model":"r-instruct-1","input":"..."}'

# example 429 response (Dedicated)
# HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
# Content-Type: application/json
# {
#   "error": "rate_limited",
#   "retry_after_ms": 15000
# }
```

oscar: minor question: do Hosted responses ever include `retry_after_ms` in body?

runtime-bot: Hosted uses standard headers (Retry-After in seconds). We do not emit `retry_after_ms` in Hosted responses. Avoid relying on body field for Hosted.

harper: good. CI bot: can you run docs-preview? I fixed the wording but want to ensure linkcheck and guide formatting is ok.

ci-bot: docs-preview: linkcheck passed, but linter flagged a missing code fence language on the JS snippet.

lena: fixed snippet fence, added the callout and a short paragraph about X-RateLimit headers not guaranteed on Dedicated. Pushed.

docs-reviewer-rajesh: re-reviewing. Once we add a tiny compatibility matrix table (Hosted vs Dedicated behavior) as a short bullet list, I'll approve. Keep it brief.

lena: done — added bullets: Hosted = Retry-After header (seconds) + X-RateLimit-Remaining/LIMIT; Dedicated = 429 with retry_after_ms in JSON body (ms) and X-RateLimit headers may be absent. Also added SDK precedence note.

ci-bot: docs-preview: all checks passed.

docs-reviewer-rajesh: approved. merging after you confirm no other changes.

lena: merging now. Thanks for the quick runtime clarification everyone.

runtime-bot: note: runtime team will not change Dedicated behavior in this release window. Any changes need RFC and migration planning.
